its data and trial index (0-based). Then we need to enforce capacity and deduplication. The requirement: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" And \"Restore chronological order in the final memory.\"\n\nSo algorithm:\n- Let capacity = case['capacity']\n- Let trials = case['trials']\n- Initialize list memory = [] (list of dicts)\n- For idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\n    entry = {\n        \"last_trial\": idx,\n        \"reflection\": trial['reflection'],\n        \"signature\": trial['signature'],\n        \"success\": trial['success']\n    }\n    memory.append(entry)\n- After processing all trials, we need to truncate to capacity and deduplicate.\nBut note: The description says \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" Possibly means that if there are multiple entries with the same signature and all are failures, we keep only the most recent. But also \"prioritize failures before successes\" suggests that when capacity is exceeded, we want to keep failures over successes, and among same priority, keep most recent.\n\nAlso \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\" might mean: For each signature, if it appears both as failure and success? Hmm. Let's read carefully: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" That suggests that for a given signature, if there are multiple entries, we keep the one with the newest reflection (i.e., largest last_trial) only if it is a failure? Or maybe we deduplicate across all entries but only for failures? Actually the phrase \"by failure signature\" could mean that we treat the signature as key, and we want to deduplicate entries that have the same signature, but only keep the newest one, and we prefer failures? The next sentence: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" That clarifies selection criteria.\n\nSo overall: We have a list of entries. We need to select up to capacity entries. Selection criteria: first keep all failures (success=false) then successes (success=true). Within each group, sort by recency descending (most recent first), but then also \"signature\" seems to be a tiebreaker? \"then recency and signature\" likely means sort by recency descending, and if recency equal, then alphabetical by signature? But recency is trial index, so unique. But okay.\n\nBut also we need to deduplicate: If multiple entries have the same signature, we should keep only the most recent (by last_trial) among them? However, the selection criteria already prioritize recency within the same success group, so if we sort and then take top capacity, the most recent entries of each signature will be chosen first. But if a signature appears multiple times, the older ones will be less recent, so they might not be selected if capacity is limited. But if capacity is large enough, we might keep multiple entries of the same signature? The deduplication requirement suggests we should not keep duplicate signatures at all. \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\" implies that the list should contain at most one entry per signature. And \"retaining the newest reflection.\" So after processing all trials, we should collapse multiple entries with the same signature to only the one with the highest last_trial. Then apply capacity bound with priorities.\n\nThus steps:\n1. Build initial list of entries from all trials (with index).\n2. Group entries by signature.\n3. For each signature, keep only the entry with the maximum last_trial (most recent).\n4. From this deduplicated set, select up to capacity entries according to priority: first entries with success=False (failures), then success=True, and within each group, sort by last_trial descending (most recent first). If last_trial equal, maybe by signature? But indices are unique, so no tie. However, for deterministic output, we can sort by (success, -last_trial) and then take first capacity.\n5. After selection, restore chronological order: sort the selected entries by last_trial ascending.\n\nReturn: memory (list of entry dicts with keys last_trial, reflection, signature, success) and signatures (list of signature strings in same order as memory entries? The schema says signatures is an array of strings. Probably the signatures of the retained entries in the same order as memory. Yes.\n\nCheck the output schema: memory is array of objects with required fields, signatures is array of strings. So we need to output both.\n\nNow, need to ensure we handle edge cases: capacity may be larger than number of entries; then we keep all deduplicated entries. If capacity is 0? In the given fixtures capacity=3. But we should handle any positive integer? Possibly zero? But assume positive.\n\nAlso note: The input trials may have a \"_case_nonce\" field, but we ignore it as per instructions.\n\nImplementation: def solve(case): return { \"memory\": memory_list, \"signatures\": sig_list }.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nNow, produce Python source.\n\nLet's write the code. Use standard library.\n\nPotential pitfalls: name collisions. Ensure we use \"case\" as argument.\n\nTest with given fixtures:\n\nCase 0: capacity=3, trials=[...]. Process deduplication: signatures: e1 appears at indices 0,3,5; e3 at 1,4; e0 at 2,6. For each signature, keep newest: e1: last_trial=5 (success=true), e3: last_trial=4 (success=false), e0: last_trial=6 (success=false). Now selection: failures first: e3 (trial4) and e0 (trial6); then successes: e1 (trial5). Since capacity=3, all three fit. Then chronological order: by last_trial: e0 (6), e1 (5), e3 (4)? Wait chronological ascending: trial index 4 (e3), 5 (e1), 6 (e0). So memory: [ last_trial:4, ref:r4, sig:e3, success:false], [last_trial:5, sig:e1, success:true], [last_trial:6, sig:e0, success:false]. Signatures: e3,e1,e0.\n\nCase 1: capacity=3, trials: signatures e2 at 0,4,5; e3 at 1,2,6; e1 at 3. Deduplicate: e2 newest trial5 (false), e3 newest trial6? Actually e3: trials 1 (true),2(false),6(false). Newest is trial6 (false). e1 trial3 (false). So entries: e2-t5-false, e3-t6-false, e1-t3-false. All failures. Selection: all three fit. Order chronologically: e1 trial3, e2 trial5, e3 trial6. So memory: [t3,e1,sig?], [t5,e2], [t6,e3], all success false. Signatures: e1,e2,e3.\n\nCase 2: capacity=3, trials: e1 at 0 false; e3 at 1,2,3,5,6? Actually trials: e1 false, e3 false (1), e3 false (2), e3 false (3), e0 false (4), e3 true (5), e3 false (6). Deduplicate: e1 newest trial0 false, e3 newest trial6 false (since trial5 is true, but we keep newest across all; trial5 true vs trial6 false: trial6 is newer, false). e0 newest trial4 false. All entries: e1-t0, e3-t6, e0-t4. All failures. Capacity 3, all fit. Chronological: e1 t0, e0 t4, e3 t6. So memory: [t0, e1, false], [t4, e0, false], [t6, e3, false]. Signatures: e1,e0,e3.\n\nCheck if any of these match expected? Not given but seems plausible.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\" might mean only deduplicate among failures? But then success entries could have duplicates? The phrase \"retaining the newest reflection\" suggests for each signature, keep the newest reflection regardless of success? I think it's across all. The priority later separates failures and successes, so we should deduplicate first to avoid having multiple entries per signature. The selection then picks failures first.\n\nLet's implement as described.\n\nNow, write the solve function. Use copy? No need to mutate case.\n\nReturn JSON serializable dict.\n\nMake sure to output exactly as schema: memory array and signatures array.\n\nNow generate Python source.\n\nImplementation code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    \n    # Build entries with trial index\n    entries = []\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        entries.append({\n            'last_trial': idx,\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\n            'success': trial['success']\n        })\n    \n    # Deduplicate by signature: keep the one with highest last_trial\n    dedup = {}\n    for entry in entries:\n        sig = entry['signature']\n        if sig not in dedup or entry['last_trial'] > dedup[sig]['last_trial']:\n            dedup[sig] = entry\n    \n    # Now we have list of unique signatures\n    unique_entries = list(dedup.values())\n    \n    # Sort by priority: failures first, then recency descending (within same success)\n    # Sort key: (success, -last_trial) i.e. False before True (0 before 1)\n    unique_entries.sort(key=lambda e: (e['success'], -e['last_trial']))\n    \n    # Take first 'capacity' entries\n    selected = unique_entries[:capacity]\n    \n    # Restore chronological order\n    selected.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n    \n    memory = selected\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in selected]\n    \n    return {\n        'memory': memory,\n        'signatures': signatures\n    }\n```\n\nCheck: For case0, dedup after dedup: entries: e3-t4, e1-t5, e0-t6? Actually e3-t4 false, e1-t5 true, e0-t6 false. Sort by success (false first): e3, e0, then e1 (true). Within false: by recency descending: e0 (t6) then e3 (t4). So sorted list: e0, e3, e1. Take first 3: all. Then sort chronological: e3(t4), e1(t5), e0(t6). That matches earlier.\n\nCase1: dedup: e1-t3 false, e2-t5 false, e3-t6 false. Sort by success: all false, recency descending: e3(t6), e2(t5), e1(t3). Selected all. Chronological: e1, e2, e3. Good.\n\nCase2: dedup: e1-t0 false, e0-t4 false, e3-t6 false. Sort: recency descending: e3(t6), e0(t4), e1(t0). Selected all, chronological: e1, e0, e3. Good.\n\nEdge case: capacity smaller than number of unique entries. Works.\n\nNow format output.\n\nWe must return exactly one JSON with key \"implementation\", value is the Python source string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Use json.dumps.\n\nI'll produce 